## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of a full retrieval pipeline, chunking strategy, and vector stores in a beginner-friendly way applicable to large-prod settings (Lyft/Discord/Adobe).

## Key Concepts

- Token-based chunking and overlap
- Local embeddings vs cloud APIs
- Vector databases and cosine similarity
- Data freshness and refresh strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal in-memory demo (no real model)
const chunks = [
  { text: 'chunk one', vec: [0.1,0.2,0.3] },
  { text: 'chunk two', vec: [0.0,0.2,0.4] }
];
function sim(a,b){ let dot = a.reduce((s,x,i)=> s + x*b[i],0); let na=Math.hypot(...a); let nb=Math.hypot(...b); return dot/(na*nb); }
const q=[0.08,0.18,0.28];
const top = chunks.map((c,i)=>({i, s: sim(c.vec,q)})).sort((A,B)=> B.s-A.s)[0];
console.log(top);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle chunking for languages with complex morphology?
- How would you test for model drift in embeddings over time?

## Purpose
This question evaluates practical planning and tool orchestration skills using LangChain for real-world data processing workflows.

## Key Concepts
- LangChain Tools, agents, and planning patterns
- Tool execution order: fetch, parse, compute
- Error handling strategies: retries with backoff, timeouts, and graceful degradation

## Code Example
```javascript
import { ChatOpenAI } from 'langchain/chat_models';
import { AgentExecutor, createOpenAIFunctionsAgent } from 'langchain/agents';
import { S3Tool } from './tools/s3';
import { DataProcessorTool } from './tools/processor';

const s3Tool = new S3Tool();
const processorTool = new DataProcessorTool();

const tools = [s3Tool, processorTool];
const model = new ChatOpenAI({ temperature: 0 });
const prompt = ...;

const agent = await createOpenAIFunctionsAgent({ model, tools, prompt });
const executor = new AgentExecutor({ agent, tools, maxIterations: 3 });

const result = await executor.invoke({ 
  input: 'Generate a daily KPI report from s3://bucket/data/daily.csv'
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this architecture to handle multiple files in batch processing?
- How would you implement testing for the planner with unreliable S3 access?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to craft brand-isolated PEFT strategies with governance for multilingual, cross-brand assistants.

## Key Concepts
- PEFT, LoRA, QLoRA; per-brand adapters; versioned KB drift; privacy and data governance; latency.

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudo-injection outline for adapters
function injectAdapters(model, brand){
  model.addAdapter('attn', {rank:8, alpha:32, type:'LoRA', quant:4});
  model.addAdapter('ffn', {rank:8, alpha:32, type:'LoRA', quant:4});
  model.selectBrand(brand);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor drift and roll back adapters? 
- How would you audit data flows per brand?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end understanding of a retrieval-augmented QA flow using embeddings, chunking, and a local vector store. It also probes data freshness considerations and how to validate recall in a practical setup.

## Key Concepts
- Chunking with overlap to preserve context
- Embeddings and cosine similarity for retrieval
- Local vector stores (FAISS-like) for fast lookups
- Data freshness via timestamps and TTL with refresh strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: basic retrieve flow
function retrieve(query){
  const qEmb = embed(query);
  const top = vecStore.topK(qEmb, 2);
  return top.map(chunk => chunk.text).join(" ");
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you tune chunk size and overlap for different docs?
- How would you measure recall and latency in this setup?
